625, 0.006649017333984375, 0.023773193359375, 0.0016937255859375, -0.002376556396484375, -0.0299530029296875, -0.00823211669921875, -0.00724029541015625, -0.00501251220703125, 0.0225982666015625, -0.01485443115234375, -0.0133514404296875, -0.001827239990234375, -0.004253387451171875, 0.01268768310546875, 0.0091094970703125, 0.0014324188232421875, 0.0218048095703125, -0.005218505859375, -0.016845703125, -0.00904083251953125, -0.0098419189453125, -0.00243377685546875, -0.0010986328125, 0.0161895751953125, 0.0028438568115234375, 0.0225982666015625, -0.0152740478515625, -0.017578125, -0.0199737548828125, -0.0027027130126953125, 0.021728515625, -0.002597808837890625, 0.012664794921875, -0.04205322265625, 0.0052337646484375, -0.014434814453125, -0.001003265380859375, -0.00510406494140625, 0.00405120849609375, -0.00714874267578125, -0.007228851318359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**\n1. Introduction\n**\n\nPoint process models offer a probabilistic framework for studying random configurations of points, as arise in fields such as spatial statistics, ecology, and neuroscience. Most classical models\u2014for instance, Poisson or Cox processes\u2014assume independence or only simple forms of dependence, limiting their ability to capture structured interactions among points. **Permanental processes** are a flexible class of random point processes that can encode attractive (clustering) dependencies, thereby capturing higher-order interactions beyond pairwise or independent models. However, these models have traditionally lacked direct means for incorporating explicit covariate information (such as experimental conditions or systematic trends), limiting flexibility and interpretability.\n\nOur goal is to bridge this gap by **introducing explicit fixed effects (covariates)** into the permanental process framework. This permits joint modeling of interpretable covariate effects and structured, nontrivial dependence among points. While models such as generalized additive models (GAMs) and Cox processes allow for covariate effects, they cannot capture the richer clustering dependencies permanentals can represent. Our main contribution is an **extension of the permanental process** that augments the basic model with fixed effects, while carefully addressing issues of identifiability and confounding between smooth and linear components.\n\n*Summary of main advances:*\n- **Formalization** of a covariate-augmented permanental process that models both higher-order dependence and explicit fixed effects.\n- **Technical construction** ensuring identifiability via an orthogonalization procedure for the nonparametric effect.\n- **Algorithmic approach** for fitting the model with practical recommendations and illustrative worked example.\n\n**Notation Summary:**  \n| Symbol | Description                                       |\n|--------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| $x$    | Location in domain $\\mathcal{X}$ (e.g., time, space) |\n| $z$    | Covariate/features at $x$ ($z \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$)     |\n| $f$    | Nonparametric effect, function on $\\mathcal{X}$       |\n| $k$    | RKHS (reproducing kernel Hilbert space) kernel function for $f$ |\n| $k^*$  | Modified kernel ensuring orthogonality to $z$         |\n| $Z$    | $n \\times p$ covariate matrix over data               |\n| $K$    | $n \\times n$ kernel Gram matrix $k(x_i, x_j)$         |\n\n> **Glossary:**  \n> - *Permanental process*: a random point process whose likelihood involves the matrix permanent, and which models attractive (clumping) interactions.  \n> - *RKHS*: a space of smooth functions defined via a kernel, enabling nonparametric adaptation.  \n> - *Permanent*: a function of a square matrix, similar to the determinant but all terms are positive; appears in the likelihood of permanental processes.\n\n**\n2. Model Specification\n**\n\n**2.1 Model Formulation**\n\nLet $\\mathcal{X}$ denote the observed domain (e.g., time interval or spatial region). Given $n$ observed points $S = \\{x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\} \\subset \\mathcal{X}$, each $x_i$ is measured with features $z_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, forming the matrix $Z = [z_1^\\top;\\ldots;z_n^\\top]$. We define the **(log-)intensity function**\n\\[\n\\lambda(x, z) = z^\\top \\beta + f(x)\n\\]\nwhere $\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$ are fixed effect coefficients, and $f:\\mathcal{X} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is a data-adaptive smooth function in the RKHS associated with kernel $k$. Unlike Cox processes, here the point configuration likelihood is governed by a **permanental process**:\n\\[\nP(S \\mid f, \\beta) = \\frac{1}{Z_{f, \\beta}} \\mathrm{per}\\left([k^*(x_i, x_j)]_{i, j=1}^n\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^n \\exp\\big(\\lambda(x_i, z_i)\\big)\n\\]\nwhere $\\mathrm{per}(\\cdot)$ is the matrix permanent, $k^*$ is the modified kernel (see below), and $Z_{f,\\beta}$ normalizes over all finite subsets of $\\mathcal{X}$ (see **Macchi 1975** for mathematical details). *In practical computation, $Z_{f,\\beta}$ is generally intractable, but for inference or optimization involving only relative likelihoods (such as for fixed-size datasets), ratios can often be computed or approximated (see Section 3.2).*\n\n**2.2 Identifiability and Modified Kernel Construction**\n\nIncluding both $f$ and $z^\\top\\beta$ introduces **non-identifiability**, since any linear trend in $f$ can be arbitrarily absorbed by $\\beta$ and vice versa (e.g., $f(x) + z^\\top\\beta = f(x) + z^\\top\\beta + z^\\top\\gamma - z^\\top\\gamma$). To **prevent confounding** between linear covariate effects and the nonparametric effect, we restrict $f$ to be orthogonal\u2014under the data distribution\u2014to all linear functions spanned by $z$. This avoids $f$ mimicking effects due to $z^\\top\\beta$.\n\n**Concrete Example:**  \nIf $z$ is a binary indicator (e.g., group label, $z_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$), then allowing $f$ unrestrictedly, the mean shift between $z=0$ and $z=1$ may be explained entirely by either $\\beta$ or by a jump in $f$. Enforcing $f$ orthogonal to $z$ ensures the group mean difference is allocated to $\\beta$, not $f$.\n\n**Kernel Modification:**  \nTo enforce this, we modify the kernel function as:\n\\[\nk^*(x, x') = k(x, x') - h(x)^\\top (Z^\\top Z)^{-1} h(x'), \\quad\\text{where } h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n z_i k(x, x_i)\n\\]\nThis guarantees that, for the observed data, the Gram matrix $K^* = (I - P_Z) K (I - P_Z)$, where $P_Z = Z(Z^\\top Z)^{-1} Z^\\top$ projects onto the span of $Z$. This construction (see Kimeldorf & Wahba, 1971) ensures identifiability of $\\beta$ and $f$.\n\n**2.3 Prior Choice**\n\nWe place a zero-mean Gaussian process prior on $f$, $f \\sim \\mathcal{GP}(0, k)$, enabling flexible nonlinear modeling, with smoothness determined by $k$. For $\\beta$, we assign $\\beta \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\tau^2 I_p)$; $\\tau^2$ tunes regularization (smaller values yield stronger shrinkage). In empirical work, cross-validation or marginal likelihood maximization can select $\\tau^2$ and kernel hyperparameters, and we discuss guidance in Section 3.4.\n\n**\n3. Methodology\n**\n\n**3.1 Generative Model and Likelihood**\n\nOur generative model assumes:  \n1. Points $S = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_n\\}$ arise from a permanental process with log-intensity $\\lambda(x,z)$ as above.\n2. Covariates $z_i$ are fixed or observed.\n3. The probability of observing $S$ is as given, with normalization constant $Z_{f, \\beta}$ integrating over all finite subsets of $\\mathcal{X}$ (see Macchi 1975; also Shirai & Takahashi, 2003 for review).  \n*Computing $Z_{f,\\beta}$ is generally infeasible except in special cases or by approximation (e.g., for fixed $n$, only relative likelihoods matter; otherwise, importance sampling or point process thinning methods may be used).*\n\n**3.2 Model Fitting and Algorithm**\n\nWe jointly estimate $(\\beta, f^*)$ by minimizing the penalized negative log-likelihood:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}(\\beta, f^*) = -\\log P(S \\mid f^*, \\beta) + \\frac{1}{2} \\|f^*\\|_{k^*}^2 + \\frac{1}{2\\tau^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2 .\n\\]\nThanks to the representer theorem (Sch\u00f6lkopf et al., 2001), $f^*$ has the finite expansion:\n\\[\nf^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k^*(x, x_i), \\quad \\text{with } Z^\\top f^* = 0\n\\]\nwhere the orthogonality constraint is enforced by construction (using $k^*$).\n\n*Optimization details:*\n- **Gradients** of $\\mathcal{L}$ with respect to $\\beta$ and $\\alpha$ can be computed analytically, noting that the gradient of the log-permanent is the matrix of marginal contributions (see, e.g., Glynn & Whitt 1992 for permanent derivatives), though the permanent itself is \\#P-hard for large $n$\u2014for small to moderate $n$ (up to 15\u201320), Ryser's algorithm gives exact computation; for larger $n$, Monte Carlo estimators (e.g., Jerrum et al., 200"}}